NEXT PERMUTATION (Very frequently asked ❗)

This one shows up in FAANG / product interviews, tests arrays + greedy logic, and interviewers love asking “explain why this works”.

📌 Problem Statement

Given an array of integers representing a permutation, rearrange the array into the next lexicographically greater permutation.

If such arrangement is not possible, rearrange it as the lowest possible order (sorted in ascending order).

👉 You must do this in-place with O(1) extra space.

🔹 Example 1
Input:  [1, 2, 3]
Output: [1, 3, 2]

🔹 Example 2
Input:  [3, 2, 1]
Output: [1, 2, 3]

🔹 Example 3
Input:  [1, 1, 5]
Output: [1, 5, 1]

🧠 Key Intuition (Very Important)

Lexicographical order = dictionary order

We want to make the smallest possible increase to the given permutation.

🚀 Algorithm (Step-by-Step Logic)
Step 1: Find the breakpoint

Traverse from right to left and find the first index i such that:

nums[i] < nums[i + 1]


✔ This is the point where we can make the number slightly bigger
❌ If no such index exists → array is in descending order → reverse it

Step 2: Find the next greater element

From the right side, find the smallest element greater than nums[i]
Swap it with nums[i]

Step 3: Reverse the suffix

Reverse all elements to the right of index i
This gives the smallest possible permutation after the swap

⏱ Complexity

Time: O(n)

Space: O(1)

☕ Java Code (Interview-Ready)
class Solution {
    public void nextPermutation(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int i = n - 2;

        // Step 1: Find the breakpoint
        while (i >= 0 && nums[i] >= nums[i + 1]) {
            i--;
        }

        // Step 2: If breakpoint exists, find next greater element
        if (i >= 0) {
            int j = n - 1;
            while (nums[j] <= nums[i]) {
                j--;
            }
            swap(nums, i, j);
        }

        // Step 3: Reverse the suffix
        reverse(nums, i + 1, n - 1);
    }

    void swap(int[] nums, int i, int j) {
        int temp = nums[i];
        nums[i] = nums[j];
        nums[j] = temp;
    }

    void reverse(int[] nums, int start, int end) {
        while (start < end) {
            swap(nums, start, end);
            start++;
            end--;
        }
    }
}

🧪 Example Walkthrough (Line by Line)
Input
nums = [1, 3, 5, 4, 2]

🔹 Step 1: Find breakpoint

From right:

5 > 4 → OK
3 < 5 → breakpoint index = 1

🔹 Step 2: Find next greater element than 3

From right:

4 > 3 → swap


Array becomes:

[1, 4, 5, 3, 2]

🔹 Step 3: Reverse suffix after index 1

Reverse:

[5, 3, 2] → [2, 3, 5]

✅ Final Output
[1, 4, 2, 3, 5]

🎯 Why Interviewers Love This Problem

Checks greedy thinking

Tests edge cases

Forces you to explain why reverse is needed

In-place logic (space optimized)
